Project Milestones
Milestone 1 – Discovery, Requirements, and Design
Students shall:
•	evaluate the current website;
•	interview sponsor stakeholders;
•	identify major user groups and journeys;
•	document functional and nonfunctional requirements;
•	identify current Salesforce-dependent website interactions;
•	collaborate with Team 2 on the shared API contract;
•	develop information architecture;
•	prepare wireframes;
•	define the MVP;
•	develop a preliminary future roadmap.
Mandatory Deliverable: Sponsor-reviewed Requirements Engineering Document.
________________________________________
Milestone 2 – Website Prototype
Students shall demonstrate:
•	primary website navigation;
•	I Need Help user journey;
•	I Want to Help user journey;
•	responsive design;
•	representative website content;
•	public-facing forms;
•	mocked or functional backend API integration;
•	initial accessibility implementation; and
•	initial usability testing.
Sponsor feedback shall be incorporated into the final development cycle.
________________________________________
Milestone 3 – Refined MVP and Future Roadmap
Students shall deliver:
•	refined website MVP;
•	completed sponsor-approved workflows;
•	responsive implementation;
•	accessibility testing;
•	usability testing;
•	API integration or validated mock integration;
•	technical documentation;
•	user/administrator documentation;
•	GitHub repository containing code and project documentation;
•	future-development roadmap; and
•	final presentation.
________________________________________
Minimum Viable Product (MVP)
The MVP shall include:
•	sponsor-approved modern website design;
•	clear separation between major visitor journeys;
•	intuitive navigation;
•	responsive design;
•	accessibility-conscious implementation;
•	core public-facing forms;
•	defined backend API contract;
•	functioning API calls or realistic mocked equivalents;
•	appropriate error handling;
•	documentation; and
•	a roadmap for functionality not completed during Phase 1.
________________________________________
Future Roadmap
Students shall identify and prioritize functionality recommended for future development.
Potential areas may include:
•	additional online services;
•	expanded volunteer functionality;
•	additional donation functionality;
•	deeper integration with the Project 2 backend;
•	additional administrative content-management capabilities;
•	enhanced accessibility;
•	multilingual support;
•	advanced engagement features; and
•	AI opportunities identified through requirements analysis.
Future functionality should be clearly distinguished from the Phase 1 MVP.
________________________________________
Definition of Success
This project will be considered successful if the student team thoroughly understands Tech for Seniors' goals and audiences, helps the sponsor determine what its future website should provide, and delivers a modern, accessible prototype that demonstrates that vision.
Success does not require implementation of every feature the organization may eventually want.
The strongest measure of success is that the sponsor can review the completed MVP and roadmap and confidently state:
“Yes. This represents the direction we want for the future Tech for Seniors website.”
